+    The announced wave is READ BACK from the recomputed schedule, never assumed to be the
+    parent's index + 1: the children are levelled by their own ``Depends on`` /
+    ``Conflicts with``, so two children of one parent routinely land in different waves and
+    a hardcoded ``k + 1`` names a wave the second one is not in. A child the reschedule
+    HELD is not announced as adopted at all, and never joins the results map —
+    :func:`_report_held` has already named it as left in-flight.
+
+    Adoption is bounded: a parent is examined once (``examined``, which the queue shares),
+    and a child already in the drive set is never re-adopted (``known``), so each adoption
+    strictly grows a finite set of on-disk bundles — the queue drains. An adopted child
+    that splits LATER, while this run drives it, is picked up by this same call in ITS wave,
+    on the same budget — a recursion bound, not a recursion reset. Either way the scope
+    stays "the children of the ids this run was given, transitively" and never becomes a
+    glob of ``results/``.
